# Calevate edge rate-limit zones — the config half of DEPLOYMENT §5.4.
#
# THIS FILE AND THAT TABLE ARE COMPARED BY A GUARDRAIL. `scripts/check_docs_drift.py`
# parses `limit_req_zone` directives out of any file named `rate-zones.conf.template`
# and diffs them against §5.4's list, in both directions and on the rate as well as the
# name. A zone added here without the doc, or renumbered in the doc without the config,
# fails CI. Change the two together.
#
# WHERE IT IS INSTALLED, AND WHY NOT WHERE THE DOC USED TO SAY. `limit_req_zone` is an
# `http`-context directive, so this file must be included inside `http {}`. Debian's
# nginx.conf auto-includes `/etc/nginx/conf.d/*.conf` there; `/etc/nginx/snippets/` (the
# path DEPLOYMENT §5 inherited from the raghava playbook) is auto-included by nothing and
# would need an explicit `include` nobody would remember to add. `vps-deploy.sh` installs
# it as `/etc/nginx/conf.d/calevate-rate-zones.conf`.
#
# `.template` for consistency with the site config beside it: both are rendered by the
# same `envsubst` step in the deploy script. This one currently substitutes nothing —
# the rates are decisions, not deployment parameters, and a per-host override would mean
# staging and production enforcing different limits with the same doc claiming both.
#
# THE KEY IS `$binary_remote_addr`, WHICH IS ONLY CORRECT BECAUSE OF real-ip.conf.
# Behind Cloudflare, `$remote_addr` is a Cloudflare edge address — a handful of them, so
# every tenant in the world would share one bucket and the limits would fire on honest
# traffic while doing nothing to an attacker. `real_ip_header CF-Connecting-IP` (see
# `snippets/calevate-origin.conf`) rewrites it to the true client before this module sees it.
# `$binary_remote_addr` rather than `$remote_addr`: 4 bytes instead of a string, which is
# what makes 10m hold ~160k distinct addresses.
#
# nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_limit_req_module.html — `zone=name:size rate=Nr/s|Nr/m`,
# leaky bucket, `burst=` queues and `nodelay` serves the burst immediately while still
# counting it. Rates below 1r/s must be expressed in r/m: nginx has no fractional r/s.
#
# THESE ARE EDGE DEFENCE, NOT THE AUTHORITY. Per-tenant concurrency and spend caps are
# enforced in the app and fail closed (DEPLOYMENT §7a). nginx protects the box from
# volume; only the app can protect client #1 from tenant #47.

# Login and token exchange. 20/min per IP is ~10x a human's worst day and still slow
# enough that credential stuffing is not worth attempting through this door.
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=auth:10m rate=20r/m;

# Admin console. One operator, a dashboard that fans out to several endpoints per view.
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=admin_api:10m rate=180r/m;

# Client dashboards. Lower than admin because there are many of them and each one is a
# browser session, not an operator sweeping every tenant.
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=client_api:10m rate=120r/m;

# Engine webhooks. Sized for the SHAPE of the traffic, not its average: a campaign
# finishing hangs up 250 calls at once (D-32's production concurrency) and every one of
# them delivers. Set too low, this drops deliveries the reconciliation poller then has to
# recover ten minutes later — the exact failure hard rule 3 exists to avoid. It is keyed
# per source IP and the engine has one, so this is a ceiling on ONE sender.
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=webhooks:10m rate=600r/m;

# IN-CALL TOOL CALLS — A SEPARATE BUCKET FROM `webhooks`, AND THE SEPARATION IS THE POINT.
# Both surfaces arrive from the engine's egress addresses, and both are keyed on
# `$binary_remote_addr`, so one zone for both is ONE bucket shared by two kinds of traffic
# with opposite shapes. `/hooks/v1/*` is POST-CALL: a campaign finishing hangs up 250 calls
# and delivers 250 webhooks in the same second (the zone above). `/tools/v1/*` is DURING a
# call — the engine's custom functions, invoked mid-conversation while a human is on the
# line. Sharing meant the hangup burst spent the allowance and the next tool call was
# refused 429 AT THE EDGE: the handler is never entered, so every deadline inside it is
# moot, the model gets a failed tool call, and the caller hears silence. Raising the shared
# zone does not fix that — it only makes the burst bigger before it eats the same
# allowance. Two zones is the fix, because the resource being protected is different.
#
# THE CEILING IS CONCURRENT CALLS, NOT COMPLETED ONES, and that is the whole derivation:
#   * concurrency ceiling: 250+ concurrent calls in production (D-32, TRD §5 — customer
#     evidence, not the marketing plan claim);
#   * per-call ceiling: a tool call follows a conversational turn (STT → model → TTS → a
#     human speaking), which cannot repeat faster than a few seconds. At a pathological
#     one call every 6s that is 10/min for a single conversation;
#   * 250 × 10/min = 2,500r/m ≈ 42r/s, which is the rate below.
# It is a real ceiling and not a rubber stamp: it bounds that one address at 42r/s against
# a process TRD §5 measures at ~1,750 acks/s, so a flood is still refused — while honest
# in-call traffic has an order of magnitude of headroom and never meets it.
#
# BURST (declared at the `limit_req`, not here) IS 250, for the reason the shape is bursty
# at all: a campaign dials its 250 calls together, so those conversations run roughly in
# lockstep and one round of tool calls can land inside a second. `nodelay` serves that
# round immediately rather than queueing it into the deadline it is racing.
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=in_call_tools:10m rate=2500r/m;

# Health endpoints. Generous enough for an uptime monitor at any sane interval, bounded
# so /healthz cannot be used as a free database-load generator.
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=health:5m rate=60r/m;

# THE BROWSER CONSOLES, and this zone is named for what uses it rather than for what it
# used to be called. It is applied in exactly four places — the `location /` of the
# marketing, client-console and admin-console vhosts, and the api vhost's
# `location ^~ /v1/public/` (D-545), whose one caller is the marketing vhost's own server
# and whose content is a rate card with no database behind it — and nowhere else. It was called
# `default` and described as "everything not matched by a more specific location,
# including the public lead-intake endpoint's IP dimension", which was never true of this
# deployment: lead intake arrives on the api vhost, whose `location /` is `client_api`.
#
# THE RATE IS 10r/s BECAUSE 1.5r/s WAS RATE-LIMITING HONEST USERS, and this is not a
# judgement call — it is in the origin's error log. At 90r/m with burst=30 a single
# operator loading one console screen produced:
#
#   [warn] limiting requests, excess: 30.206 by zone "default", client: <redacted>,
#     server: app.calevate.tech, request: "GET /_next/static/chunks/9926-....js"
#   [warn] limiting requests, excess: 30.380 by zone "default", ...
#     request: "GET /c/<slug>/messaging-consent?_rsc=..."
#
# — the burst was full and the queue 30 deep, on ONE person navigating. The cause is what
# a Next App Router console is: one screen is dozens of hashed JS chunks, and every
# `<Link>` in the sidebar fires an RSC prefetch (`?_rsc=`) the moment it is rendered. That
# is a browser being normal, not an attack, and a limiter that cannot tell them apart is
# refusing the wrong traffic. Requests were being answered 429.
#
# 10r/s sustained with a burst of 120 absorbs a cold page load and its prefetch fan-out
# while still bounding the box. What it costs: a flood from ONE address now gets 600
# requests a minute of static-asset service instead of 90. That is the right trade at
# this layer — these vhosts serve documents and cached assets, not database work; the
# expensive surfaces (`auth` 20r/m, `client_api` 120r/m, `admin_api` 180r/m) are on the
# api vhost, are untouched by this, and are where a flood would actually hurt.
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=browser:10m rate=600r/m;

# Refuse with 429, not nginx's default 503. 503 says "the server is broken"; 429 says
# "you are going too fast", which is the true statement and the one a client's HTTP
# library will back off on rather than retry immediately.
limit_req_status 429;
limit_req_log_level warn;
